From 5bcfb0b67ac45d39c8e3d96948315ced8371ee5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:19:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] x11: answer TARGETS and text/uri-list for drags, refuse
 the rest

A drag of file names (NSFilenamesPboardType) offers the drop target
text/uri-list in its type list, but the SelectionRequest handler only
answered the string targets.  A target that asked for TARGETS, as ICCCM
requires and as GTK does before anything else, or for text/uri-list got
no reply at all, not even a refusal, so it waited and then gave up:
files dragged out of a GNUstep application could not be dropped on a
file manager, a browser or a mail composer.

The handler now answers three ways:
- TARGETS: the atoms of the types the drag offers (TARGETS itself,
  UTF8_STRING, STRING and TEXT for a string, text/uri-list for files);
- text/uri-list: the files as file URIs, one per line, each line ending
  in CRLF (RFC 2483);
- anything it can't give: SelectionNotify with property None, at once.
The string targets are answered as before.

Testing: Tests/x11/xdndfilenames.m plays the drop target from a second
X connection.  It makes a GNUstep window the owner of XdndSelection on
the backend's own connection, as starting a drag does (the X server
sends a SelectionRequest to the client that set the owner), asks for a
target with XConvertSelection while the application waits for events,
and reads back the property and the SelectionNotify.  With two files on
the drag pasteboard, one with a space in its path, it checks that
TARGETS lists text/uri-list and TARGETS and not UTF8_STRING, that
text/uri-list gives exactly
"file:///tmp/xdnd%20test/a%20b.txt\r\nfile:///tmp/c.pdf\r\n", and that
UTF8_STRING is refused at once; with a string, that UTF8_STRING still
gives the string, that TARGETS lists UTF8_STRING and not text/uri-list,
and that text/uri-list is refused at once.  That covers each new answer,
the format of the list, and guards the existing string path and against
offering types the drag doesn't have.  It needs a display but no window
manager and no drag.

On a private Xvfb, each backend loaded from a GNUstep.conf whose user
Library held only that build: without this change 7 of the 9 checks
fail (2 pass: the string answer, and one check that holds when nothing
is answered); with it all 9 pass.  The whole suite: 293 passed, none
failed, against 286 passed and 7 failed on master; pdfps.m aborts the
same way on both, in the installed libs-gui snapshot's font roles.

Not covered by the test: a real drop.  Under GNOME Shell (X11, Xvfb),
dragging a file from a GNUstep application onto Nautilus still fails
with this change alone: GTK ignores the drag because the XdndEnter
message says version 2 ("Ignored old XdndEnter message").  The next
change speaks version 3; with both, the file is copied into the folder.

AI assistance: this change, its test and this description were written
with Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
